      "text_raw": "Let us now look again at the\nactual transresistance A\nR\nand at the input resistance\nR .\nin\nFor very large values\nof the load resistor, A\nR\nbecomes R itself. The gain\nLc\nA includes the gain of the\nR\ntransistor g r , and is\nm DS\ntherefore quite high. It is the\nhighest transresistance gain\nthat can be achieved by\na single-transistor cascode.\nFor that purpose, the load\nresistor must be larger than\nthat very same transresis-\ntance R .\nLc\nFor these large load resistances, the input resistance of the transistor itself becomes infinity.\nThis is expected as the load resistor is also infinity – no current can pass through the transistor.\nThe resistance seen by the input current source thus becomes resistor R , which is actually its\nB\noutput resistance. It is thus by no means small, as suggested by the 1/g low load resistances.\nm",
      "text": "Let us now look again at the actual transresistance A R and at the input resistance R . in For very large values of the load resistor, A R becomes R itself. The gain Lc A includes the gain of the R transistor g r , and is m DS therefore quite high. It is the highest transresistance gain that can be achieved by a single-transistor cascode. For that purpose, the load resistor must be larger than that very same transresistance R . Lc For these large load resistances, the input resistance of the transistor itself becomes infinity. This is expected as the load resistor is also infinity – no current can pass through the transistor. The resistance seen by the input current source thus becomes resistor R , which is actually its B output resistance. It is thus by no means small, as suggested by the 1/g low load resistances. m"
