  "commentary_text": "Let us now look again at the actual transresistance A R and at the input resistance R . in For very large values of the load resistor, A R becomes R itself. The gain Lc A includes the gain of the R transistor g r , and is m DS therefore quite high. It is the highest transresistance gain that can be achieved by a single-transistor cascode. For that purpose, the load resistor must be larger than that very same transresistance R . Lc For these large load resistances, the input resistance of the transistor itself becomes infinity. This is expected as the load resistor is also infinity – no current can pass through the transistor. The resistance seen by the input current source thus becomes resistor R , which is actually its B output resistance. It is thus by no means small, as suggested by the 1/g low load resistances. m",
